(Title) Tj
T*
(Translating Pain Management Knowledge Into Home-Based Ambulatory Care: Implementation) Tj
T*
(Outcomes From a Cluster Randomized Trial) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Community-based older adults often experience inadequately managed chronic pain due to) Tj
T*
(organizational and provider-level barriers. We conducted a pragmatic trial in 22) Tj
T*
(ambulatory nursing services comparing individualized provider support, digital education,) Tj
T*
(and control conditions \(total 190 baseline, 144 follow-up due to 24% attrition\). The) Tj
T*
(individualized provider intervention improved documentation of pain assessments compared) Tj
T*
(to controls; however, this improvement in process measures did not translate to meaningful) Tj
T*
(changes in medication appropriateness or patient-reported pain severity at follow-up. The) Tj
T*
(digital education platform was adopted inconsistently by participating providers and) Tj
T*
(showed similarly limited impact on primary clinical endpoints. Qualitative interviews) Tj
T*
(revealed that substantial structural barriers--including insufficient staffing levels, lack) Tj
T*
(of financial reimbursement mechanisms for pain management activities, and competing) Tj
T*
(clinical demands--constrained implementation of any intervention across all study arms.) Tj
T*
(These pragmatic findings underscore that isolated provider- or system-level interventions) Tj
T*
(may be insufficient without concurrent policy reforms that enable dedicated time and) Tj
T*
(resources for comprehensive pain management in ambulatory elderly populations.) Tj
